1
00:00:05,232 --> 00:00:11,467
Fanny Price is the quiet poor relation at Mansfield Park, and nobody waits to hear what she thinks.

2
00:00:11,883 --> 00:00:16,704
This guide covers the novel and a version where Edmund lets her finish her sentence.

3
00:00:17,636 --> 00:00:21,403
Hello, and welcome to Unwritten Chapters, Study Edition.

4
00:00:21,746 --> 00:00:26,229
In the next few minutes, I'll walk you through Mansfield Park, by Jane Austen.

5
00:00:27,940 --> 00:00:34,832
Fanny Price is ten when she's sent from her crowded home in Portsmouth to live with rich relatives at Mansfield Park.

6
00:00:35,229 --> 00:00:40,335
Her bossy aunt, Mrs Norris, never lets her forget she's the poor relation.

7
00:00:40,747 --> 00:00:44,021
Only her cousin Edmund treats her with real kindness.

8
00:00:44,334 --> 00:00:52,185
Years later, while her uncle, Sir Thomas, is away at his estate in Antigua, the charming Crawfords arrive from London.

9
00:00:52,638 --> 00:00:56,628
The young people decide to put on a daring play called Lovers' Vows.

10
00:00:57,069 --> 00:01:03,410
Edmund falls for the witty Mary Crawford, while Fanny quietly watches and refuses to act.

11
00:01:04,171 --> 00:01:07,608
Next, who's who, so you can keep everyone straight.

12
00:01:09,421 --> 00:01:10,547
Fanny Price.

13
00:01:10,711 --> 00:01:14,364
Shy and gentle, but quietly firm about what's right.

14
00:01:14,931 --> 00:01:16,180
Edmund Bertram.

15
00:01:16,582 --> 00:01:19,711
Her kind cousin, who plans to become a clergyman.

16
00:01:20,251 --> 00:01:22,154
Sir Thomas Bertram.

17
00:01:22,478 --> 00:01:25,853
Her stern uncle, the owner of Mansfield Park.

18
00:01:26,649 --> 00:01:27,864
Mrs Norris.

19
00:01:28,111 --> 00:01:31,281
Her bossy aunt, who is always unkind to her.

20
00:01:32,267 --> 00:01:33,446
Mary Crawford.

21
00:01:33,685 --> 00:01:36,453
A lively, witty young woman from London.

22
00:01:37,235 --> 00:01:38,448
Henry Crawford.

23
00:01:38,789 --> 00:01:41,780
Mary's charming brother, who loves to flirt.

24
00:01:43,268 --> 00:01:45,045
Now for the story.

25
00:01:45,332 --> 00:01:48,306
Here's how it unfolds, chapter by chapter.

26
00:01:49,162 --> 00:01:50,202
Chapter one.

27
00:01:50,437 --> 00:01:51,529
The East Room.

28
00:01:52,311 --> 00:01:56,489
Fanny Price was ten years old when she came to live at Mansfield Park.

29
00:01:57,268 --> 00:02:01,600
Her own family in Portsmouth was large, poor and noisy.

30
00:02:02,013 --> 00:02:06,578
Her cousins thought she was dull, and Aunt Norris kept telling her to be grateful.

31
00:02:06,968 --> 00:02:10,930
One day her cousin Edmund found her crying on the attic stairs.

32
00:02:11,162 --> 00:02:12,859
She missed her brother William.

33
00:02:13,187 --> 00:02:16,434
So Edmund found her paper and helped her write him a letter.

34
00:02:16,779 --> 00:02:18,420
Years passed.

35
00:02:18,700 --> 00:02:23,206
Sir Thomas sailed to Antigua, and the lively Crawfords arrived from London.

36
00:02:23,587 --> 00:02:29,398
The young people decided to act a play called Lovers' Vows, and Fanny refused to take part.

37
00:02:29,809 --> 00:02:37,106
Then her cousin Tom said he'd invite Charles Maddox, a young man they barely knew, to play Anhalt opposite Mary Crawford.

38
00:02:37,411 --> 00:02:42,414
The next morning, Edmund knocked on the door of the East room, Fanny's little sitting room.

39
00:02:42,974 --> 00:02:45,519
He said he must take the part himself.

40
00:02:46,196 --> 00:02:48,336
"Can you think of anything else, Fanny?"

41
00:02:49,227 --> 00:02:54,283
"No," she said slowly, "not immediately, but..."

42
00:02:54,998 --> 00:02:57,069
In the book, he doesn't wait.

43
00:02:57,441 --> 00:02:59,035
This time, he did.

44
00:03:00,032 --> 00:03:01,115
Chapter two.

45
00:03:01,383 --> 00:03:02,614
Fanny's Answer.

46
00:03:03,438 --> 00:03:06,109
Edmund sat down by the window and waited.

47
00:03:06,817 --> 00:03:09,036
Fanny's heart was beating fast.

48
00:03:09,294 --> 00:03:12,121
She wasn't used to anyone waiting for her opinion.

49
00:03:12,533 --> 00:03:16,805
Her geraniums stood on the windowsill, and her books were piled on the table.

50
00:03:17,210 --> 00:03:20,626
There was no fire, because Aunt Norris said she didn't need one.

51
00:03:21,029 --> 00:03:24,650
"You were against the play from the start," she said at last.

52
00:03:24,893 --> 00:03:26,701
"You said my uncle wouldn't like it.

53
00:03:27,056 --> 00:03:31,073
I'm sorry for Miss Crawford, but I'm sorrier to see you give in."

54
00:03:31,550 --> 00:03:35,497
"Last night Miss Crawford told me she wasn't hopeful about the play.

55
00:03:35,846 --> 00:03:38,552
She said acting with a stranger would be horrid.

56
00:03:38,909 --> 00:03:42,432
If you asked her honestly, perhaps she'd rather stop too."

57
00:03:43,413 --> 00:03:45,797
Edmund looked at her for a long moment.

58
00:03:46,178 --> 00:03:47,302
Then he smiled.

59
00:03:48,051 --> 00:03:49,595
"Fanny," he said, "you're quite right.

60
00:03:49,807 --> 00:03:50,959
I think I knew it when I came in.

61
00:03:51,264 --> 00:03:55,061
I just didn't want to hear it."

62
00:03:55,902 --> 00:04:00,898
He'd walk down to the Parsonage after breakfast, he said, and talk to Miss Crawford.

63
00:04:01,866 --> 00:04:02,975
Chapter three.

64
00:04:03,144 --> 00:04:04,459
The Green Curtain.

65
00:04:05,180 --> 00:04:07,935
Mary Crawford laughed when Edmund explained.

66
00:04:08,712 --> 00:04:11,138
"Oh, thank goodness," she said.

67
00:04:11,319 --> 00:04:14,328
"I was dreading those rehearsals with Mr Maddox."

68
00:04:14,986 --> 00:04:18,104
She gave up the part of Amelia that very afternoon.

69
00:04:18,871 --> 00:04:23,019
Without an Amelia or an Anhalt, the play began to fall apart.

70
00:04:23,491 --> 00:04:28,530
Tom asked his sister Julia to play Amelia, and Julia said certainly not.

71
00:04:29,031 --> 00:04:31,424
Within a week, even Tom was bored.

72
00:04:31,676 --> 00:04:34,127
He'd always lost interest in things quickly.

73
00:04:34,475 --> 00:04:36,681
Mrs Norris was the most upset.

74
00:04:37,196 --> 00:04:40,415
She'd ordered yards of green cloth for the stage curtain.

75
00:04:40,899 --> 00:04:45,548
In the end she folded it up and carried it home, because she said it would be useful.

76
00:04:46,203 --> 00:04:49,295
A few days later, a carriage came up the drive.

77
00:04:49,559 --> 00:04:51,908
Sir Thomas was home early from Antigua.

78
00:04:52,277 --> 00:04:55,153
He found his family sitting quietly in the drawing room.

79
00:04:55,477 --> 00:04:57,678
Edmund told him the whole story.

80
00:04:58,100 --> 00:05:01,026
"Fanny was right from the beginning," he said.

81
00:05:01,519 --> 00:05:03,300
The Last Page.

82
00:05:03,912 --> 00:05:06,976
Sir Thomas looked at his niece for a long time.

83
00:05:07,500 --> 00:05:11,435
He gave orders that a fire should be lit in the East room every day.

84
00:05:11,838 --> 00:05:15,037
That winter, the little room was warm at last.

85
00:05:15,588 --> 00:05:21,045
Fanny sat by the fire with her books and her plants, and Edmund came up most mornings to talk.

86
00:05:21,553 --> 00:05:26,200
He told her about the sermons he was planning, and the village parish he'd soon look after.

87
00:05:26,668 --> 00:05:29,714
He asked her opinion, and he always let her finish.

88
00:05:30,546 --> 00:05:33,386
Edmund was slow to understand his own heart.

89
00:05:33,743 --> 00:05:35,769
Austen's Edmund always was.

90
00:05:36,196 --> 00:05:41,569
But one bright spring morning, he looked up from his book and saw Fanny smiling by the window.

91
00:05:41,904 --> 00:05:45,902
And he wondered why he'd never noticed how much he looked forward to that smile.

92
00:05:46,753 --> 00:05:50,719
Next, the big ideas, the ones that come up in class.

93
00:05:52,148 --> 00:05:53,511
Conscience.

94
00:05:54,006 --> 00:05:58,012
Fanny's quiet sense of right holds firm when everyone pushes her.

95
00:05:58,656 --> 00:06:00,420
Charm and principle.

96
00:06:00,870 --> 00:06:05,077
The Crawfords are clever and fun, but they lack firm principles.

97
00:06:05,900 --> 00:06:06,900
Upbringing.

98
00:06:07,214 --> 00:06:11,504
Sir Thomas taught his daughters fine manners, but not how to be good.

99
00:06:12,514 --> 00:06:14,041
Home and belonging.

100
00:06:14,512 --> 00:06:18,008
The poor relation slowly becomes the heart of Mansfield.

101
00:06:19,778 --> 00:06:22,675
Here are two lines worth quoting in an essay.

102
00:06:23,615 --> 00:06:26,456
"No, indeed, I cannot act."

103
00:06:27,283 --> 00:06:31,635
Fanny refuses to take a part in the play, however hard they push.

104
00:06:32,438 --> 00:06:38,118
"We have all a better guide in ourselves, if we would attend to it, than any other person can be."

105
00:06:39,139 --> 00:06:44,014
Fanny tells Henry Crawford to follow his own conscience, not her advice.

106
00:06:45,113 --> 00:06:47,701
Next, let's explore a what-if.

107
00:06:49,501 --> 00:06:54,801
In an alternative scenario, what if Edmund waits for Fanny to finish her sentence?

108
00:06:55,449 --> 00:06:57,725
So what could have happened?

109
00:06:58,550 --> 00:07:03,127
Path A. Edmund agrees to play Anhalt, as he does in the book.

110
00:07:03,458 --> 00:07:06,846
It could happen: He's already half made up his mind.

111
00:07:07,102 --> 00:07:11,238
But this time, he's waiting to hear Fanny out.

112
00:07:11,669 --> 00:07:14,249
Path B. Fanny gives in and agrees to act as well.

113
00:07:14,669 --> 00:07:19,299
It could happen: Everyone is pushing her, and she hates to seem unkind.

114
00:07:19,566 --> 00:07:22,094
But Fanny never goes against her conscience.

115
00:07:22,835 --> 00:07:27,708
Path C. Fanny tells Edmund what she really thinks, and he listens.

116
00:07:28,102 --> 00:07:33,064
This one fits the characters: Edmund trusts Fanny's judgement more than anyone's.

117
00:07:33,254 --> 00:07:36,157
And Fanny tells the truth when it really matters.

118
00:07:37,022 --> 00:07:38,237
Which would you pick?

119
00:07:38,498 --> 00:07:41,199
Pause and decide, then see if you agree.

120
00:07:43,135 --> 00:07:50,797
The verdict: Path C. Austen's Fanny is shy but never false, and Edmund values her opinion.

121
00:07:51,150 --> 00:07:53,616
Once she speaks, he'd see she's right.

122
00:07:55,480 --> 00:07:57,644
So what actually happens in the book?

123
00:07:58,481 --> 00:08:02,270
In the book, Edmund agrees to play Anhalt opposite Mary.

124
00:08:02,688 --> 00:08:08,601
Sir Thomas comes home early in the middle of a rehearsal and burns every copy of the play he can find.

125
00:08:09,035 --> 00:08:15,309
Later, Maria Bertram marries the rich, dull Mr Rushworth, then runs off with Henry Crawford.

126
00:08:15,828 --> 00:08:20,593
Mary's careless reaction finally opens Edmund's eyes, and he marries Fanny.

127
00:08:21,373 --> 00:08:23,323
Why did Jane Austen choose it?

128
00:08:23,974 --> 00:08:30,809
Austen shows that quiet, steady principle matters more than charm, and that Fanny was right all along.

129
00:08:32,435 --> 00:08:35,747
Before we go, here's a little about who wrote it.

130
00:08:36,577 --> 00:08:42,886
Jane Austen was born in 1775 in Steventon, Hampshire, where her father was the rector.

131
00:08:43,293 --> 00:08:48,763
She wrote Mansfield Park between 1811 and 1813 at her cottage in Chawton.

132
00:08:49,070 --> 00:08:55,049
It came out in 1814 without her name, and the first printing sold out within six months.

133
00:08:55,385 --> 00:08:59,676
Two of her brothers were officers in the Royal Navy, like Fanny's brother William.

134
00:09:01,512 --> 00:09:08,454
Sir Thomas owns property in Antigua, a Caribbean island where sugar plantations were worked by enslaved people.

135
00:09:09,040 --> 00:09:14,925
Britain had banned the slave trade in 1807, but slavery itself went on in its colonies.

136
00:09:15,478 --> 00:09:19,887
When Fanny asks her uncle about the slave trade, the room falls silent.

137
